<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Territory of Alsace-Lorraine</h1><p class="st-meta">1871 CE – 1918 CE · capital Strasbourg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The territory taken by the German Empire from France in 1871, governed directly from Berlin until it returned to France in 1918.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> of 1871, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, to the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a>. Instead of joining an existing state, the region became a Reichsland, an imperial territory under the emperor's direct authority.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Strasbourg</a>, and from 1879 by an imperial governor (Statthalter) in Strasbourg, the first being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-von-manteuffel">Edwin von Manteuffel</a>. Tens of thousands opted for French citizenship and left. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invested in Strasbourg's university and new imperial quarter. A constitution of 1911 created a regional parliament, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saverne-affair">Saverne Affair</a> of 1913, when soldiers abused civilians, revealed lasting tensions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany's</a> defeat in November 1918, councils briefly proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-alsace-lorraine">Republic of Alsace-Lorraine</a> before French troops entered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1919 returned the territory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where it still keeps some distinct local laws.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-territory-of-alsace-lorraine.jpg" alt="Imperial Territory of Alsace-Lorraine" width="320" height="400"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wappen_Deutsches_Reich_-_Elsass-Lothringen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1871 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Strasbourg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edwin von Manteuffel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alsace-Lorraine, Reichsland Elsass-Lothringen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=imperial-territory-of-alsace-lorraine" aria-label="Imperial Territory of Alsace-Lorraine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4860 480 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Territory of Alsace-Lorraine lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5180 736L5300 622M5180 736L5054 728M5180 736L5180 736"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5054 728h0M5180 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5180 736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5180 736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Strasbourg. <a href="/atlas?year=1894&amp;focus=imperial-territory-of-alsace-lorraine">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
